  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 303 PM MDT Wed Oct 21 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nToday over Southwestern Montana there remains Southwest flow aloft \nwith weak zonal flow (west-east) over North-central, and Central \nMontana. Due to slightly stronger warm air advection today \ntemperatures will rebound to above seasonal averages. Across North-\ncentral and Central Montana it will be dry with a low chance for \nshowers across Southwestern Montana today. See the Forecast \nConfidence & Scenarios Section for details. On Thursday there \nremains southerly flow aloft and an upper-level trough associated \nwith a Pacific Cold front begins to move over North-central, \nCentral, and Southwestern Montana by Thursday evening. This will \nallow temperatures to remain above seasonal averages with mostly dry \nweather until Thursday evening. Thursday evening into Friday morning \nprecipitation will gradually spread over North-central, Central, and \nSouthwestern Montana. \n\nOn Friday the upper-level trough merges with a shortwave trough \nmoving south from Canada. This combined with the cold front that \nmoved through will bring widespread precipitation and cooler \ntemperatures (below seasonal averages) to the area. Due to the \ncold front that moved through Thursday evening into Friday morning\nsnow-levels will lower and the precipitation that falls in \nmountain areas on Friday will transition from rain to snow by \nFriday evening. On Friday there is an increasing pressure gradient\nand strong low-level and mid-level winds. This will bring windy \nconditions to North-central Montana on Friday. On Saturday an \nupper-level ridge begins to build in over North-central, Central, \nand Southwestern Montana. This will allow the area to dry out with\ncool temperatures lingering. \n\nOn Sunday the upper-level ridge remains over North-central, Central, \nand Southwestern Montana. This will keep dry and cool (below \nseasonal averages) weather for the area on Sunday. Monday through \nTuesday an upper-level trough moves over North-central, Central, \nand Southwestern Montana. This will bring cooler temperatures and\nwidespread precipitation to the area on Monday and Tuesday. -IG\n\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nFor total liquid precipitation Thursday night through Friday night \nthere is greater than an 80% chance for one half inch of liquid \nprecipitation or greater along and south of the Montana Highway \n200/US Highway 87 Corridor between Great Falls and Lewistown. For \ntotal liquid precipitation Thursday night through Friday night there \nis a 30 - 60% chance for one half inch of liquid precipitation \nacross North-central Montana north of the Montana Highway 200/US \nHighway 87 Corridor between Great Falls and Lewistown. For total \nliquid precipitation Thursday night through Friday night there is a \n60 - 90% chance for one inch of liquid precipitation or greater \nacross Central and Southwestern Montana.\n\nAcross most of the mountains of North-central, Central, and \nSouthwestern Montana there is greater than a 60% chance for 2 \ninches of snow or greater from Thursday night through Friday \nnight. Thursday night through Friday night there is a 50 - 80% \nchance for 4 inches of snow or greater across the Madison and \nGallatin ranges and the Little Belts. Friday afternoon into early \nSaturday morning there is a high chance that at least some lower-\nelevation locations across North-central, Central, and \nSouthwestern Montana will see a few flakes of snow. There is a bit\nof uncertainty regarding how low snow levels will get which would\ninfluence how much snow will be observed at lower elevations.\nRight now there are some models suggesting 1 to 3 inches in Great\nFalls but there is a lot of uncertainty given how warm this system\nis compared to what would be more productive for higher snowfall\namounts. -IG/thor",
